@@s001 Least squares loss
@@s002 dato:the data (xᵢ, yᵢ),due:the predictions f(xᵢ),ora:the move now,ok:the loss
@@s003 dato:the errors,no:sum first (wrong),ok:square first (right)
@@s004 Point (${P[i][0]}, ${P[i][1]}): the model predicts f1(${P[i][0]}) = ${f(P[i][0])}. Error ${P[i][1]} − ${f(P[i][0])} = <b>${err}</b>, squared <b>${q}</b>.
@@s005 dato:the 3 data,due:f1(x) = 3x² − 2x + 1,no:errors (residuals),ok:the line that passes through them all
@@s006 The three data (2, 5), (3, 7), (4, 9).
@@s007 The model f1(x) = 3x² − 2x + 1: a parabola.
@@s008 Loss = sum of the squares divided by N = 1265 / 3 ≈ <b>421.7</b>. Each red segment is an error: the square makes the long ones weigh a lot.
@@s009 The data lie exactly on the line y = 2x + 1: with that model the errors would all be 0, and the loss 0. The parabola has the wrong shape.
@@s010 due:|e| absolute value,ok:e² square,ora:an error to compare
@@s011 dato:the data,ora:trial parameter,ok:the minimum
@@s012 Least squares loss
@@s013 A number that says how much a model gets wrong on the data. It is computed error by error: first the square, then the sum, then the mean. Here: the formula symbol by symbol, the order of the operations, a worked example, why precisely the square, and the loss seen as a function of the parameters. Exercise 3 of week 1 (Evaluating Models, Grasple).
@@s014 The formula and its symbols
@@s015 The <b>loss</b> (in Italian "perdita", or cost function) is a single number that summarises how much the model gets wrong on <b>all</b> the data. The smaller it is, the better the model fits. That of least squares (<i>least squares loss</i>) is:
@@s016 Symbol
@@s017 Meaning
@@s018 the <b>input</b> of point <span class="m">i</span>: it enters the function
@@s019 the <b>label</b> of point <span class="m">i</span>: the true answer
@@s020 the <b>prediction</b> of the model
@@s021 <span class="m">y<sub>i</sub> &minus; f(x<sub>i</sub>)</span>
@@s022 the <b>error</b> (residual) on point <span class="m">i</span>
@@s023 the number of <b>points</b> in the dataset, not of models
@@s024 <span class="m">&sum;<sub>i=1</sub><sup>N</sup></span>
@@s025 sum over <span class="m">i</span> from 1 to <span class="m">N</span>: it runs through the points
@@s026 It is read from inside out. For each point: put x<sub>i</sub> in the model, compare with the truth y<sub>i</sub>, square the error. Then sum all the squares and divide by how many there are. The result is the <b>mean of the squared errors</b>, which in the metrics of week 11 is called MSE (<i>mean squared error</i>).
@@s027 The loss does not say <b>where</b> the model errs, only <b>how much</b> in total. It serves to compare models: with two models two losses are computed, and the lower one wins.
@@s028 N counts the points (question asked in chat)
@@s029 The index of the sum is <span class="m">i</span>, the same as <span class="m">x<sub>i</sub></span> and <span class="m">y<sub>i</sub></span>: so <span class="m">N</span> counts the points, not the variables or the functions. With two models the formula is applied <b>twice, separately</b>, and one obtains two losses to compare.
@@s030 The formula filled in column by column (exercise 4 of the test)
@@s031 Power first or sum first?
@@s032 The most common doubt about the formula (also asked in chat): is the <sup>2</sup> applied before or after the Σ? The answer lies in the brackets. The <sup>2</sup> is attached to the bracket (y<sub>i</sub> − f(x<sub>i</sub>)), which is <b>inside</b> the summation: it belongs to each single term. So first the square of each error, then the sum.
@@s033 Power first
@@s034 Read from the inside outwards: bracket &rarr; square &rarr; sum &rarr; division by <span class="m">N</span>.
@@s035 Square first (right)
@@s036 <span class="m">(&minus;4)<sup>2</sup> + (&minus;15)<sup>2</sup> + (&minus;32)<sup>2</sup></span>
@@s037 Sum first (wrong)
@@s038 <span class="m">(&minus;4 &minus; 15 &minus; 32)<sup>2</sup></span>
@@s039 <span class="m">= (&minus;51)<sup>2</sup> = 2601</span>
@@s040 The order is not a convention: it changes the meaning. Summing first, the positive and negative errors <b>cancel out</b>, and a model that errs everywhere can get 0, that is "perfect". The square on each error makes them all positive before summing them.
@@s041 If you see <span class="m">(&sum; &hellip;)<sup>2</sup></span>, with the bracket enclosing the sum, then yes, one sums first. In the loss there is none.
@@s042 Errors that cancel out, and squares that do not
@@s043 Worked example
@@s044 Data <span class="m">(2, 5), (3, 7), (4, 9)</span>: the first number is the input, the second the label. Model <span class="m">f<sub>1</sub>(x) = 3x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 2x + 1</span>. The formula is applied point by point, exactly in the order of the section above.
@@s045 Loss of f1
@@s046 dato:the data,ora:the move now,ok:result
@@s047 Evaluate the model on <b>each</b> input: three points, three predictions.
@@s048 Error of each point: true label minus prediction.
@@s049 Square of each error, one by one.
@@s050 Only now the sum.
@@s051 Mean: divide by <span class="m">N = 3</span>, the number of points.
@@s052 Shows the loss of f2(x) = 2x&sup2; + 4x + 3
@@s053 Predictions <span class="m">19, 33, 51</span>. Errors <span class="m">&minus;14, &minus;26, &minus;42</span>. Squares <span class="m">196, 676, 1764</span>. Sum <span class="m">2636</span>. <span class="m">L &asymp; 878.7</span>. So <span class="m">f<sub>1</sub></span> is better.
@@s054 A high loss does not mean wrong calculations
@@s055 The data lie exactly on the line <span class="m">y = 2x + 1</span>, which would have loss 0. Both models are quadratic, so they have the <b>wrong shape</b>. The errors of <span class="m">f<sub>1</sub></span> grow systematically (&minus;4, &minus;15, &minus;32): it is the sign of a problem of the model, not of noise. It will be called <b>bias</b> in week 6.
@@s056 All the numbers verified with NumPy.
@@s057 The errors, drawn
@@s058 Why the square and not the absolute value
@@s059 To prevent the errors from cancelling out it would be enough to remove the sign, that is to use the <b>absolute value</b> |yᵢ − f(xᵢ)|. So why precisely the square? Three reasons.
@@s060 <b>1. It weighs large errors more.</b> An error of 0.5 costs 0.25, one of 3 costs 9. The model is pushed above all to avoid big mistakes. The other side of the coin: a single anomalous datum (<i>outlier</i>) can move the result a lot.
@@s061 <b>2. It is smooth.</b> The graph of e² is a curve without corners, that of |e| has a point at 0. Smooth curves can be minimised with derivatives and, in the case of linear models, with an exact formula (the normal equations of week 5).
@@s062 <b>3. It has a single minimum.</b> For models linear in the parameters the squared loss is a "bowl" with a single bottom, as the next section shows: there are no traps.
@@s063 The mean of the absolute value does not disappear: it is called MAE (<i>mean absolute error</i>) and is used as an evaluation metric in week 11.
@@s064 Two ways of removing the sign
@@s065 The loss depends on the parameters
@@s066 Up to here the model was fixed and its loss was computed. Now the point of view is reversed: the <b>data are fixed</b> and it is the <b>parameters</b> that change. For every choice of the parameters one obtains a different loss, so the loss is a <b>function of the parameters</b>: it is written L(w).
@@s067 Learning means finding the parameters where L(w) is minimal: it is the meaning of "minimizing the least squares" (slide 36). With a single free parameter everything can be seen in one graph: the loss draws a <b>parabola</b>, because it is a sum of squared terms in the parameter, and the minimum is its bottom.
@@s068 With two parameters (w₀ and w₁) the parabola becomes a 3D "bowl", with many parameters a bowl in many dimensions. The bottom is found with linear algebra (<a href="w05-minimi-quadrati.html">least squares</a>) or by going down step by step along the slope (<a href="w14-reti-neurali.html">gradient descent</a>).
@@s069 Every line has its own loss: the parabola L(w₁)
